(fleet_budget); registered in API_INDEX under area cost, so /api/area/cost lists two feeds. cost-feed.test.mjs. */ /* 1.48.0 ARCH-SELF-MODEL-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; owner directive 2026-10-19: "the system needs to know more about its own architecture (self reference)"): the fleet can now be asked what it is. GET /api/architecture (and /api/architecture/worker/<name>, /table/<name>, /impact/<worker>, ?q=, ?full=1) and the page /architecture answer from the fleet code and live topology graph (FLEET-GRAPH-1, qnfo-graph), the service registry, the heartbeats and the cron table: every worker with its layer, purpose, version, last tick, schedules, routes, databases, service dependencies in both directions and the tables its code reads and writes; the databases, the fleet-exec loops, how a change travels from a work claim to a canaried deploy, the lanes of authority, the registries that hold the fleet's memory, the graph's own findings, and self: the sources it read, the ones it could not, and what it cannot see. Platform area page "Architecture". Open and read-only; names and counts only. */ /* 1.47.0 CREDIT-HALT-ONLY-1 (2026-10-19, owner directive): "AI credit runs out in about N days" is never a queue card and never urgent. The prepaid balance is topped up automatically, so a low balance halts nothing. The only credit card is a balance at or below zero AND gateway calls actually failing on credit in the last 6 hours (llm_gateway_log 402/403 or insufficient/quota/payment/billing/credit errors). A runway is display context only. */ /* 1.46.1 GA4-PROPERTY-MISMATCH-1 (2026-10-19, pillar reach, agent_issues 2935, code task ct_im2935): ga4Ingest read the default property 486253526, which holds 1-2 page views a day for qnfo-publications.pages.dev, while the Zaraz tag G-LV7RHRVW6R on qnfo.org, q08.org, qwav.tech, ipatent.me and empoweringchange.today sits in another property. With no pinned ops_config ga4_property_id it now lists the properties the service account can read (Analytics Admin accountSummaries), reads the web data streams of each, takes the property whose stream carries the measurement id, and saves it as ga4_property_id; when none does, the ledger row ga4-ingest-<day> says so (property_warning) and the default is read. The codeagent draft looked for measurementId on the property summary, which has none, and sat behind a numeric check that was always true. */ /* 1.46.0 KPI-THRESHOLDS-1 + CODE-FLOW-PR-SPLIT-1 + OWNER-REQUEST-RECEIPT-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; agent_issues 2727 and 2941, and the owner-queue metrics owner_queue_fleet_cards_open and owner_dispatched_items_7d): (1) the Metrics tab opens with Service levels: every registered metric against its threshold with a state (BREACH, WARN, NO_READING, OK, NO_THRESHOLD), the time in breach and its area, BREACH first and longest first, and the items past their SLA by class with the age of the oldest (v_metric_trigger_state, metric_registry, metric_history, v_sla_over_120m); GET /api/kpi gains service_levels and sla and loses every last_7d and all_time key (code tasks are one 30-day split, posts per channel 30 days). (2) The Code loop flow splits "closed, unmerged" by what happened to the pull request (code_task_pr_links): closed never opened a PR, PR closed unmerged, and superseded, which stays out of the failure share; one SQL (CODE_OUTCOME_SQL) feeds the flow and the Metrics tab. (3) The card the owner_request_requires_card trigger writes when an owner-request issue closes (source owner-request-issue) is a receipt: listed apart as Closed for you with its issue number, never in the count, the banner or an area queue; the gate already grades such a card "notice" (OWNER-NOTICE-GATE-1, migrations/2026-10-19-owner-notice-gate.sql), so the two metrics do not count it; this release changes only the page. */ /* 1.45.0 AREA-PAGES-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; owner directive 2026-10-18 "refactor the fleet dashboard and API feeds by functional area", repeated 2026-10-19 as "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD BY FUNCTIONAL AREA"): the page half of AREA-FEEDS-1. (1) AREA_REGISTRY is the one table for the six areas; FEED_AREAS, AREA_DEFS, AREA_STARS, AREA_ROOT and AREA_FLOW are derived from it, and it holds what each area's page, tabs, cards, switches and owner-queue cards are. (2) The navigation is the areas (Overview, Cost, Research, Reach, Autonomy, Reliability, Platform, Command line); Metrics, Content and Changelog sit under their area behind a sub-bar. (3) /area/<id> answers for all six areas (/area/platform was a 404): the area's queue cards, constraint and breaches, pipelines, the cards that sat on the overview (Keep investing? under Cost, Progress and the social switch under Reach, System health under Reliability, the owner-queue switch under Autonomy), its reads with their lag, and a live refresh (?frag=1&rev=, 204 while nothing moved). The overview keeps the queue, one row per area (a named bottleneck or a stopped switch shows on the row) and the two headline cards. (4) GET /api by_area and /api/area/<id> name each area's page. (5) AREAS2_CSS was defined and never sent, so the per-area rows, the switch list and the summary link shipped unstyled on 1.44.5; it is in the page head now. (6) /kpi and /api/kpi answered 500 on 1.44.5 (a half-built KPI-THRESHOLDS-1 rebuilt the KPI document as if it were a map of metrics); the route serves the document as computed, and agent_issues 2727 stays open. (7) "AREA-MAP <id>:" banners mark 40 regions of this file by area (README, Code map). Additive except the overview's Pipelines, Progress and Pipeline controls cards, which moved to their areas. */ /* 1.44.7 FALLBACK-HEARTBEAT-PROOF-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy): each scheduled run writes fleet_heartbeat (ok = the guarded fleet_tick fallback insert ran without error), so fm-single-heartbeat proves the second heartbeat producer is alive on a day the primary never misses instead of failing 24 hours after the last fallback tick. */ /* 1.44.4 AREA-FEEDS-5 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; deployment test of 1.44.3): GET /api was the one read that carried an ETag but no body `revision` and no ?since=, so a client that cannot send a header - the reason ?since= exists - could not dedupe the 13 KB index, and the index's own note ("a read with ?since=<revision> answers {not_modified:true}") was false about itself. It now goes through rtRead like every other read: body revision, ?since=, and an ETag over the content (endpoints plus the area grouping) instead of a path-and-schema string. The catalogue row now says "public 15s, ETag". Additive: no field removed, the ETag is strictly stronger. */ /* 1.44.3 AREA-FEEDS-4 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; closeout review of 1.44.2): the area feed omitted `blind`, the list of sources areasModel could not read, while /api/areas carries it. With a table unreadable and no breach rows the feed would have answered breaches:0 and status ok - a false green in the one read whose job is truthful state, which is exactly what AREAS-1 forbids ("an unreadable source is named in blind, never shown as zero"). The feed now carries blind (the same list /api/areas sends) and degraded:true when it is non-empty. Additive only: no field removed, status still agrees with /api/areas so the two cannot disagree. */ /* 1.44.2 AREA-FEEDS-3 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; red-team of the live 1.44.1): GET /api/area/<id> answered 500 {"error":"URI malformed"} for a malformed percent sequence (/api/area/%E0%A4%A, /api/area/%C0%80) because decodeURIComponent throws and the id was decoded unguarded, while every pre-existing read compares the raw path and answers 404. An anonymous poller can send one by accident, so a 500 here is noise in the fleet's own error accounting. The decode is now guarded and an unreadable escape falls back to the raw segment, which takes the 404 path. Everything else red-teamed clean: trailing slash, case, empty and nested ids, ?since= with and without the rev: prefix, ?full=1 size, the revision holding steady while data.age_min moved 22 -> 23 over 65 s (the nested clock field really is excluded), and no owner field in the body. No route or field removed. */ /* 1.44.1 AREA-FEEDS-2 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; two defects found by verifying 1.44.0 in production, both mine): (a) the page claim was wrong - humanHtml renders areas through focusRowsHtml, not areaSectionsHtml, which is dead code with no call site, so editing it changed nothing anyone sees; that edit is reverted and the link that was actually missing (an area row to its machine feed) is added to focusRowsHtml in both branches, clean areas included. (b) by_area[].live meant "every member read is live" while the feed's own live:true means "this feed is computed at request time"; two meanings under one name in one response, so the grouping field is renamed all_members_live and the feed keeps live. No route, field or behaviour removed. */ /* 1.44.0 AREA-FEEDS-1 (2026-10-18, owner directive "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD AND API FEEDS BY FUNCTIONAL AREA. FEEDS SHALL ALWAYS BE UPDATED AUTOMATICALLY FROM LIVE DATA AND REAL-TIME"): the read surface is now organised by functional area, as data, not as a convention. (a) Every row of API_INDEX names the one functional area it serves (cost, research, reach, autonomy, reliability, platform), whether it is computed live at request time or from the rebuilt fleet state, and the worst-case lag a client must assume (source, lag_s). GET /api returns that grouping as areas + by_area, so a client can enumerate an area's feeds instead of guessing. (b) GET /api/area/<id> is one functional area as its own feed: its star metric and trend, its breaches ranked, the constraint it is believed to be stuck behind, its pipelines, and the reads that belong to it with their declared lag. It is computed from D1 at request time and revalidates like every other read. (c) Fixed a shadowed route: GET /api/ga4 answered 500 "GA4 property ID not set" live because an older handler for the same path stood in front of the GA4-DATA-API-1 ledger read and called the retired stub helpers; the stale handler and its two dead helpers are removed, so the ledger read answers and revalidates. (d) A snapshot-backed read now refreshes itself: /api/actions and /api/integration read fleet_dashboard_state without ever scheduling a rebuild (only /api/state did), so a poller could keep receiving a 15-minute-old answer; both go through freshState, and /health states which reads are snapshot-backed and which are live. (e) the area feed states the age of the oldest metric it reports (data.age_min) because those metrics are refreshed by other workers and on 2026-10-18 ranged from minutes to 18 hours; RT_CLOCK strips age_min at any depth, so the age never moves the revision while the values and the count do. No route removed, no field removed. */ /* 1.43.2 ALL-READS-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "FIX ALL AUTOMATICALLY"): the last two open reads join the same contract, so every open read now revalidates. GET /api/loop and GET /api/changes kept returning no-store with no validator; both now go through rtRead and carry a revision, an ETag and ?since= (on /api/changes ?since= still means the timestamp it always meant: a revision is eight hex characters and can never equal an ISO date, so the two meanings cannot collide). With this the whole open read surface - 18 endpoints plus /health - answers the same way. */ /* 1.43.1 KPI-CLOCK-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; COMPACT-READS-1 follow-up, found by verifying the deploy): /api/kpi answered 200 instead of 304 because its body carries as_of and activity_as_of, built from Date.now() on every build, so two isolates (or one cache expiry) could never agree on a revision. The route suite missed it because kpiData hands back an isolate-cached object, so both calls in one process saw the same as_of; rt-api.test.mjs now slices the digest block and asserts a clock field never moves the revision and a content field always does (J2-J6), which fails without this fix. Both fields join RT_CLOCK. No behaviour change beyond the 304. */ /* 1.43.0 COMPACT-READS-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "FIX ALL AUTOMATICALLY" after the RT-API-1 report): the three items that report listed as deliberately not done or as a sharp edge. (a) GET /api/areas was 101 KB and 55 KB of it was fullLever, the same text the clipped lever already carries at ten times the length; the API now sends the compact row and keeps the whole source text behind ?full=1, schema_version fleet-areas/v3 (the page is untouched: it renders from areasModel, not from the API). (b) GET /api/changelog sent all 267 releases (164 KB) to every poller that wanted the newest ten; it is now one page of 20 with ?limit=1..200, ?offset= and ?limit=all, schema_version changelog/v2, and it revalidates like the other reads instead of no-store. (c) the digest no longer strips fields by a blanket name rule: RT_CLOCK is the explicit list of clock fields and everything else is part of the identity, and rt-api.test.mjs now mutates a NON-clock field of every revalidating read and requires the ETag to move, so over-stripping fails the suite instead of silently serving a stale 304. (d) ?since=<revision> is now on /api/areas, /api/human and /api/changelog as well as /api/live, every revalidating read returns X-Revision, and a body field `revision`, so a client that cannot send a header can still dedupe. No route removed; no field removed without a parameter that restores it. */ /* 1.42.1 RT-API-FRAGMENT-REV-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; RT-API-1 follow-up, found by verifying the deploy): the first deploy revalidated the JSON reads exactly as designed (/api/live, /api/areas, /api/human answered 304 with no body) but the page fragment did not: the same code path sets an ETag and the edge serves X-Generated-At while the ETag never arrives on text/html, so the poll's If-None-Match could not match and it kept re-downloading 12 KB. The fragment now carries X-Content-Revision and accepts it back as ?rev=, answering 204 with no body while nothing has changed; If-None-Match and 304 remain honoured. No other route is touched. */ /* 1.42.0 RT-API-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD AND REAL-TIME APIS"): measured on the live worker before the change, no read answered with an ETag, every read answered Cache-Control: no-store and GET /api was a 404, so a poller could neither ask "did anything change?" nor enumerate what it may call; /api/areas alone was 122 KB and the page re-downloaded and re-rendered its fragment every 60 s whether or not anything had moved. Now (a) GET /api is the read API's own index: every public read with its auth, schema_version, cache policy and what makes it change (API_INDEX, one table instead of scattered knowledge); (b) GET /api/live is one consolidated snapshot for pollers (state verdict and probe counts, queue open/urgent/oldest, the five areas with their constraint and lever, the invest verdict, a content revision) so a poll is one call instead of four; (c) a read carries a weak ETag computed over its CONTENT digest (time-derived fields excluded, or nothing would ever revalidate), honours If-None-Match with 304 and an empty body, and states a cache policy: public reads may be reused 15 s, a response carrying owner fields never may (private, no-store, Vary on the credential); (d) /api/areas, /api/human and /api/live answer 304, and /api/live also accepts ?since=<revision> for clients that cannot send a header; (e) the page's 60 s poll sends the fragment's ETag and on 304 leaves the DOM untouched (no re-render, no lost focus or scroll); (f) /health advertises api-index, live-snapshot and conditional-reads. No route was removed and no payload field was dropped. */ /* 1.41.0 GA4-DATA-API-1 (2026-10-18, pillar reach): the GA4 stubs are replaced by a real Data API read (service account GA4_SA_KEY, RS256 JWT in WebCrypto) into reach_signals source ga4 once a day, ledger cloud_ops_events ga4-ingest-<day>, GET/POST /api/ga4, watchmaker op ga4-ingest; a refused grant writes its reason, never a zero. */ /* 1.40.1 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-2 (2026-10-17, pillar security/observability): the request and cron error catches now log at error severity with structured JSON (they previously returned the error without logging, so unhandled request/cron failures were invisible in the logs); the remaining string-concatenated console.log/console.error calls are converted to structured JSON.stringify so logs are searchable. No control-flow changes. */ /* 1.40.0 REACH-DIRECT-TRAFFIC-1 (2026-10-17, pillar reach; owner directive: see which papers and links are accessed directly from Cloudflare analytics/logs, not just social-media clicks): the /kpi Reach section gains "Top papers, opened directly" and "Top links, opened directly" (7 days), read from reach_signals source cf-rum-human (Cloudflare Web Analytics RUM page loads, bots excluded) grouped per paper slug and per page, excluding the fleet's own surfaces so the owner's own browsing is not reader traffic. This is the direct-traffic complement to the social-post click counts under "Most popular posts". */ /* 1.39.2 OWNER-VOICE-CAP-PARITY-1 (2026-10-17, pillar reach, agent_issues 2729): portfolioWeeklyCap falls back to PORTFOLIO_SOCIAL_WEEKLY_CAP_DEFAULT = 21, the cap qnfo-social weeklyCap runs (PAPER-ROLLUP-1), instead of the literal 2; the 2 failed the cadence check on any normal week, so the 05:00Z guard wrote social_paused=1 on 10-15, 10-16 and 10-17. wrangler.toml sets SOCIAL_ALLOW_Q08=1 so the 1.38.3 q08-link rule does not flag links Q08-OPEN-1 made allowed. // 1.39.1 (2026-10-17): a lever is clipped on a word boundary, so it can no longer end mid-word ("about 4k neur..."), and the root-cause sentence names the titles it is expected to clear in quotes. */ /* 1.39.0 AREAS-2 (2026-10-17, pillar autonomy; owner directive EXECUTE REFACTOR: organise the dashboard by functional area and action, not by data source, without overload, extraneous information or redundancy): the 15-row breach wall and the five tiles are replaced by five constraint rows, each carrying the cost of its constraint, its owner, its lever, the lever type (code loop / your decision / fleet loops) and its remedy history; every other breach moves to a deterministic /area/<id> page, which replaces 15 per-row Diagnose model calls; AREA_ROOT promotes the known root cause (verification monoculture) over the tautological probe count and names the breaches it explains; a Pipeline controls card shows kill-switch state and when it was set; the duplicate Subscribers progress bar is removed; GET /api/areas carries schema_version fleet-areas/v2. */ /* 1.38.0 AREAS-1 (2026-10-17, pillar autonomy; owner request: refactor the dashboard around functional areas and action items, with bottlenecks and progress, without overload or redundancy): five area tiles (cost, research and content, reach and growth, autonomy loop, reliability and security) each with one star metric and its 14-day trend replace the four-number strip; breaching metrics are listed per area, worst three first (priority, then gap), each with owner, sparkline, direction and a Diagnose control that fills the command line; a Pipelines panel shows issue age, code loop, research queue and outreach with the bottleneck stage named; the Open data card and the business-case section are removed (every figure is a tile, a verdict reason or a sparkline); GET /api/areas. / 1.37.6 SCHEMA-ALTER-PROBE (2026-10-14, pillar core; the qnfo-ops 2.48.2 pattern, PR 1258): liveScheduled reads pragma_table_info(worker_schedules) and runs ALTER ... ADD COLUMN created_on only when the column is missing, so no failed D1 statement reaches Workers telemetry on each call; the schema block's outer catch logs. */ /* 1.37.5 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-qnfo-fleet-dashboard-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2532, pillar security): the 14 swallowed catches log; the nine server-side ones write console.error JSON with worker and where (the ALTER TABLE on worker_schedules skips the expected duplicate-column error), and the five in the browser scripts write console.debug. Control flow unchanged. */ /* 1.37.4 FLEET-JOURNEYS-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2554, pillar core): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists fleet-journeys, the qnfo-fleet-control journey tick (journey_runs runner cf, every 20 minutes). */ /* 1.37.3 SLA-120M (2026-10-14, owner directive: no SLA exceeds 120 minutes, 10-minute resolution): the loop-signal warn SLA is 120 min (was 720) and its breach comment speaks in minutes; the watchmaker stuck limits are in minutes (stuck_min) and every SLA-type limit is 120 min (inbound mail 72 h, code-loop PR green 6 h and waiting 48 h, attention decisions 240 h, transformation levers 24 h, q08 review and intents 48 h); lookback windows keep their length (Gmail runs and person merges 30 days, outreach-learner allocation 48 h). */ /* 1.37.2: the outreach-learner stuck count excludes jr-outreach-lookup-* runs, as it excludes jr-outreach-learner-*. */ /* 1.37.1 OUTREACH-LOOKUP-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-14, pillar reach): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists outreach-lookup, the qnfo-cloud-ops email lookup that runs every 120 minutes (SLA-120M-3). */ /* 1.37.0 CONTENT-PIPELINE-1 (2026-10-14, pillars research and reach; owner directive "visibility onto the content creation process end-to-end on fleet dashboard"): a Content tab (/content, JSON /api/content, open) shows every idea, paper, revision, erratum and post on one production line from qnfo-audit v_content_pipeline (stage, state, time in stage, SLA verdict), with papers.qnfo.org release-gate verdicts (living-paper) and q08.org signal-to-piece gates (q08-signal); the cron writes metric papers_live_failing_release_gate hourly. */ /* 1.36.1 COMPAT-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-13, agent_issues 2412, pillar autonomy): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists compat-exposure, the qnfo-ops worker_compat_state snapshot (cadence 12h). ATTENTION-STOP-GRADE-1 (agent_issues 2467): a STOP decision is graded on its prediction, the other social channels' pooled 7-day attention per item, not on its own channel, whose share it set to 0 (that read 0 -> 0, flat, for every STOP). */ /* 1.36.0 MAIL-REPLY-INSTRUCTION-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner screenshot "Fail."): "reply 984 always show me draft first before sending" was offered for sending as the email text. reply <id> <text> now sends the text as written only when it reads as an email (greeting, several lines or a sign-off); otherwise the text is the owner's instruction and the fleet writes a complete draft of that reply, shown first with Send reply. The Reply button on inbox cards and in mail <id> runs that draft directly. */ /* 1.35.0 MAIL-DRAFT-COMPLETE-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner: "when prompted the system must generate full, complete, ready-to-send drafts. do not burden the user with manual effort or input"): drafts no longer carry [[FILL]] gaps. The fleet reads the facts itself (the qnfo.org public profile, the owner identity document in the private store, and the GitHub record of any file or commit the thread names) and the model writes a complete reply, including any requested statement, from those records only; a sentence with a title, unit or placeholder no record supports is dropped in code; every draft carries Send reply. */ /* 1.34.0 MAIL-DRAFT-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner: "NEVER INVENT TITLES OR BUSINESS UNITS OR FABRICATE ANY OTHER INFORMATION ... REQUIRING MULTIPLE BACK-AND-FORTH PROMPTS"): a request to draft a reply no longer goes to the general question prompt. It finds the message by address, id or inbox card, gives the model the stored thread and only the owner's name, turns every fact on record nowhere into a [[FILL: ...]] gap (a title or unit the thread lacks is replaced in code), writes the sign-off in code (name and sending address), shows real line breaks, and offers Edit, then send (the full reply in the command line, up to 8000 characters) and, with no gap left, Send reply; "send it" sends the last draft; a reply holding a gap is refused. */ /* 1.33.0 OWNER-DOC-API-1 (2026-10-12, agent_issues 2373 EMAIL-VOICE-DRAFTER-1, pillar personal): GET and POST /api/owner/doc/<key> let a fleet worker read an owner document and propose a new version (or create a seed the owner then edits) through this worker, the only one bound to the private store; the caller is a service binding that names itself in props (qnfo-email), the loop token or the owner session. 1.31.3: main (1.30.1-kpi) merged into this branch; the version moves so the push is ahead. 1.31.2: the inbox reply and spam paths log their caught errors to Workers Logs (WORKERS-STANDARDS-GUARD-1). 1.31.1: INBOX-REPLY-1 (below) and OUTREACH-SLA-CYCLE-1 ship in the same PR as 1.31.0; the version moves so each push is ahead. */ /* 1.31.0 DASHBOARD-FULL-ACCESS-1 (2026-10-12, pillar autonomy; owner: "the fleet dashboard is my one-stop assistant to the Quniverse and shall have full access to all resources"): every ask since 09:33Z answered "the model service answered HTTP 401": a secret added to this worker in the Cloudflare dashboard (GA4_SA_KEY) made a version that copied the bindings without props, so SVC_QNFO_AI lost caller = qnfo-fleet-dashboard and qnfo-ai refused it. A model leg that qnfo-ai refuses (401/403/5xx or a network error) now runs the same Workers AI model through the new direct AI binding, so a lost prop or a router outage no longer silences the command line. The console gains mail (search the inbox, read a message in full; owner session), service bindings to qnfo-email, qnfo-cloud-ops and qnfo-fleet-control for call, and sql reads of q08 and the private identity D1 (owner session; identity stays read-only). info shows the signed-in owner up to 1500 characters of each message and tells a visitor to sign in for subject and text. MAIL-ASK-1 (same day, owner: "what do they say? don't waste my time"): "WHAT'S UP WITH RESEARCHLETTERS EMAILS?" and the follow-up "AND WHAT DO THEY SAY?" reached the model with no message at all (MAIL_ASK_RX had no plurals, "researchletters" did not name theresearchletters.org, a follow-up named no sender); plurals, say/said/content, a card domain typed without "the" or the TLD, and a follow-up with mail cards in the queue now read the messages, and the owner's context carries 1500 characters of each (was 300). A journal call for papers ("We invite your research article", "submit your full-length article", "fits our journal scope", an ISSN line) is a solicitation, not a person owed a reply (MAIL_SOLICIT_RX, parity with qnfo-cloud-ops 1.25.2), so it leaves the owner queue and is listed as handled. INBOX-REPLY-1 (same day, owner: "THE FLEET DASHBOARD IS THE QNFO INBOX"): inbox cards carry Reply (fills "reply <id> " in the command line), Read and Spam; "reply <id> <text>" sends through qnfo-email /send as the address the mail was written to (rowan.quni@qnfo.org by default) with "Re: <subject>", marks the message replied and closes its queue rows (fresh code: it speaks in the owner's name); "spam <id> ..." marks messages spam and adds the sender to email_sender_policy junk; the card no longer says "Open the qnfo inbox". */ /* 1.30.1: hosts fold to their property by domain, probe clicks left out, 64-character campaign tags matched, ingest notes carry no upstream error text. 1.30.0 FLEET-KPI-1 (owner request 2026-10-12, pillar reach): a Metrics tab (/kpi, JSON /api/kpi, open to everyone) computed from qnfo-audit at request time: open issues by priority and category, opened and closed per day, median age, oldest, closed with evidence in 24 h and 7 d, code-loop tasks; bot-filtered page views per property and day, outreach actions and events per channel and day, posts per channel, the most popular posts with links, subscribers, mentions and citations; the Buffer channels, posts and per-post statistics; a value nothing measures is null and shown as not measured; the daily reach ingest adds a per-host row with page loads and visits. */ /* 1.29.4 CMD-AI-TIMEOUT-1 (agent_issues 2316): cmdModelCall sends timeout_ms just under its own abort and names a 504 router_timeout; WATCHMAKER-TICK-LEDGER-1 (2363): tick-sequence-ledger entry, duplicate dangling-bindings-census entry removed; REPORT-CARD-CATCHUP-1 (2054): the weekly card is caught up on any tick and deduped only against measured rows. */ /* 1.29.3 WORKERS-BEST-PRACTICES-1: every LOOP_TOKEN check (6 POST routes + the machine flag) uses constEq instead of !==/===. */ /* 1.29.2 MODEL-ID-AUDIT-1 (2026-10-11): qnfo-ai routes only the ids in its MODELS table; llama-3.3-70b-instruct-fp8-fast is not one, so the third ask leg silently became a second deepseek-v4-flash. Order is now glm-5.3-flash, deepseek-v4-flash-wa, gpt-oss-120b: the Workers AI legs probe at 2-3 s through qnfo-ai, the DeepSeek API leg at 13 s. */ /* 1.29.1 CMD-ASK-BUDGET-1 (2026-10-11): 1.29.0 raised CMD_AI_BUDGET_MS to 40 s, but the job runs in ctx.waitUntil, which the runtime cancels 30 s after the response, so the first live ask (cmd-517f7562d37f1041ef0a) never wrote its fallback and stayed running until the page gave up at 45 s; the budget is back under that limit (24 s, hedge 4 s) and a suite asserts it stays below 28 s. 1.29.0 CMD-ASK-FAST-1 (2026-10-11, pillar autonomy; owner: "Fix fleet command line"): every command-line and Ask question since 2026-10-06 ended in the stored-data fallback ("The AI model did not answer just now (it timed out)", 7 of the last 7 rows): ASK_MODELS named glm-5.2, a model qnfo-ai no longer lists, so the first call burned its slot and glm-5.3-flash, asked second with the remaining budget, timed out at 13 s. The models are now raced with a staggered start (ASK_HEDGE_MS, first answer wins; each failure names its model in cmd_log.error), the budget is 40 s, and a question about Q08 reads published_pieces from the q08-signal D1 (new read binding Q08) into the model context and into the no-model fallback, which also says plainly when its stored data does not cover the question instead of answering with a status dump. 1.28.2 1.28.2 NOZ-DOI-1 (2026-10-10, pillar reach): no deposit-service (10.5281) data is collected, scored or shown: reach rows, the impact KPI, the identity-weekly lookups and the watchmaker op are removed, and entity_map ignores that DOI prefix. 1.28.1 NO-MEMORY-FACTS-1 (owner directive 2026-10-10): the public Ask and command prompts state that no fact, name, figure or history may come from outside CONTEXT. */ /* 1.28.0 CONSOLE-BUDGET-WRITE-1 (agent_issues 2072, 2026-10-08, pillar autonomy): the console accepts sql! writes to fleet_budget (RULE-8-RETIRED-1: the fleet sets its own spend limits), with the same WHERE, row cap, console_backups copy and confirm button as every other write. 1.27.0 DASHBOARD-AUTONOMY-FIRST-1 (2026-10-08, agent_issues 2171, pillars autonomy and research): SCALE_BACK no longer puts "Decide: scale the fleet back" in the owner queue (spend caps are the fleet's, charter rules 10 and 11; /api/human carries spend_decision "fleet"), and a question about research, the pipeline, papers or the queue reads research_queue facts into Ask and its no-model fallback; the 15-minute cron is the second direct producer of qnfo-audit.fleet_tick (D1-TRIGGER-DEPTH-1). 1.26.8 (2026-10-08, pillar autonomy, PROBE-RUNNER-WATCHMAKER-1 #2076): WATCHMAKER_OPS time-gated-verification reads the consumer's own verification rows (cadence 2h) and probe-cadence reads the qnfo-cloud-ops scheduler_state stamp. 1.26.2 (2026-10-06, pillar cost): the ask-corpus-sync watchmaker entry says the feeder runs lean, not stopped, while an ai_spend cap is breached (BUDGET-SOFT-ROUTE-1). 1.26.1 (2026-10-06, pillar autonomy, OWNER-NO-LOOSE-ENDS-1): SAI-KAIZEN-GRADIENT-1 (#2054): computeSai kaizen = 1 / (1 + step x open issues), a gradient at every backlog size (the linear clamp read 0 above 20 open issues), in parity with qnfo-observability; WATCHMAKER-BY-PERSON-PRECISION-1 (#1726): code-task-merge counts a closed task as needing a person only when a person or session closed it or landed the change (23 of 53 counted rows were runner closures); WATCHMAKER_OPS gains owner-steps-watch (#2058, qnfo-cloud-ops) and dangling-bindings-census (#2051, qnfo-fleet-control). 1.26.0 UTM-CLICK-LEDGER-1 (transformation lever T7.9, pillar reach): the daily reach ingest reads qnfo-graph utm_clicks (written by qnfo-gateway 3.10.0 for every utm-tagged HTML page load, bot/human class, no cookie or IP) through the GRAPH binding into reach_signals source utm (channel = utm_source, entity campaign = utm_campaign, metric clicks_human / clicks_bot), so a social post or digest joins to the visits it caused; before the first tagged load the missing table is a note, not an error. 1.25.0 1.25.0 FLEET-CHANGELOG-1 (owner request 2026-10-06, pillar autonomy): a Changelog tab (/changelog, JSON /api/changelog, open to everyone) of what the fleet ships for itself: each worker version whose major or minor number is above every earlier one, and each worker Cloudflare created after 2026-09-29, from the canonical deploy ledger deployment_history; described from the commit on main that set the version (no model call), tagged with charter pillar and the public hostnames it serves, filterable (autonomy, public-facing, new workers, major, pillar); patch fixes are counted, not listed; an autonomy trend strip reads metric_history (watchmaker_index, metrics_in_breach, code_task_success_rate_30d, session_execution_ratio_30d); personal-plane releases in full only for the signed-in owner; synced in the existing 15-minute cron into qnfo-audit.fleet_changelog, heartbeat changelog-tick in WATCHMAKER_OPS. 1.24.1 (2026-10-06): WATCHMAKER_OPS ask-corpus-sync for qnfo-ai-search 2.2.8 ASK-CORPUS-FEEDER-1 (agent_issues 2029, carried for session 01KzS1 and PR 676); 1.24.1 is above PR 671's 1.24.0 (VERSION-AHEAD-1). 1.24.0 ATTENTION-LOOP-1 (2026-10-06, pillar reach; owner directive 2026-10-06 07:44Z: granular human attention per outreach channel and item, audit automatically, do more of what gets noticed, promote better or stop what is not): the reach ingest writes bot-filtered per-item rows (source cf-rum-human: pageviews per page, paper and referrer host; external_pageviews and continuation_pageviews per page) and the Buffer per-post metrics qnfo-cloud-ops collects (source buffer, per channel post); a daily scorecard after the ingest (attention_scorecard: items, human engagements, attention per item, referred visits, impressions per channel over 7 and 28 days; pages and papers noticed from outside; subscribers by source) with a verdict per social channel (INSUFFICIENT, STOP, PROMOTE, IMPROVE, KEEP, RETEST after 28 days) and a share in [0, 1] written to ops_config attention_channel_share, which qnfo-social multiplies into the owner caps and uses to order content by attention; every change is an attention_decisions row with evidence, a prediction and a 7-day verification that grades attention_decision_efficacy_30d; metrics attention_events_7d, attention_per_post_7d, pages_noticed_7d, attention_channels_stopped; GET /api/attention; WATCHMAKER_OPS attention-scorecard; no new cron, binding or model call. 1.23.2 (2026-10-06): the transformation-loop watchmaker entry names qnfo-fleet-control 0.4.132 (TP-1a parity, open-issue contract count, wave-exit stall); 1.23.1 (2026-10-06, pillar autonomy): WATCHMAKER_OPS measures TRANSFORMATION-LOOP-1 (qnfo-fleet-control 0.4.132: the transformation program engine, hourly; counted when its tick is stalled or an open CODE-TASK-NEEDS-SESSION-1 row names a lever only a session can land, so session dependence is measured as it falls). 1.22.4 (2026-10-06): the agent-issues remediation lane no longer dispatches to the retired qnfo-kaizen (WORKER-RETIRE-WAVE-2, #1756; noAction with a note) and qnfo-kaizen leaves CON_SVC; 1.22.3 HUMAN-AUDIENCE-1 (2026-10-06, pillar reach; owner directive 2026-10-06 "do real humans find and visit the pages, and keep reading"): two hourly metrics from one bot-filtered RUM read over 7 days, external_referred_pageviews_7d (page loads on the public hosts whose referrer is outside every fleet domain; direct loads excluded, that is where renders, probes and the owner land) and continuation_pageviews_7d (page loads whose referrer is another public page: the reader clicked on), both with falsifiable triggers; breakdown in cloud_ops_events human-audience-<day>, series in reach_signals cf-rum-human; WATCHMAKER_OPS measures the qnfo-social per-channel drain (DAILY-DISTRIBUTION-1) and the radar-hub signal intake (SIGNAL-INTAKE-SOURCES-1). 1.22.2 TEXT-QUALITY-LOOP-1 (agent_issues 1895, pillar reach): GET /api/generators lists the 12 text generators from D1 qnfo-audit.text_generator_inventory with writer family, non-model gate, cross-family read and outcome metric, and each one's latest run from a fixed query in GENERATOR_RUNS; open to everyone, no new cron, no model call. 1.22.1 FLEET-CONSOLE-REDOS-1: dispatch inputs are parsed one anchored k=v token at a time (linear, max 10), so crafted input can no longer backtrack exponentially (CodeQL js/redos alerts 331/332 on PR 623); 1.22.0 FLEET-CONSOLE-1 (owner request 2026-10-05: full control, input and remediation from the dashboard, every detail and decision without leaving it): the command line gains issue <id> (row, triage, contracts, verifications, claims, code task), backlog, info <queue item> (a Details button on every queue card), tasks, locks, prs, pr <n>, runs (open reads); logs <worker>, sql [db] <select>, prio/owner/comment/codetask/reopen (owner session); close/wontfix with evidence, sql! writes backed up to console_backups first, worker calls, workflow dispatch, deploy via canonical-deploy.yml, merge and close PRs (owner + fresh code, confirm button); the loop token cannot run console actions; auto-run owner actions are logged in cmd_log; 1.21.7 OUTREACH-WEEKDAY-1 (#1940): the outreach queue's freshness counts weekday (UTC) hours, because qnfo-cloud-ops sends Monday to Friday only (it read STALE every weekend); REACH-INGEST-BACKFILL-1 (#1711): after yesterday's reach ingest, each tick backfills the most recent of the previous 7 days that has no reach-ingest row (one day per tick), so a day the cron missed (10-04) needs no manual ingest; 1.21.6 REFRESH-INFLIGHT-1 (#1942): every 15-minute cron since 2026-10-04 14:45Z ended exceededWallTime (900 s, cpu 0, no subrequest; 4 internalError an hour, 93 of 93 scheduled events in 24h) because it awaited a refresh promise left by a cancelled fetch invocation that could never settle; reuse of that promise is now age-bounded (120 s), only its owner clears it, and the cron runs its own refresh behind a 150 s bound and falls back to the saved state, so every later step of scheduled() runs; HUMAN-SEV-HIGH-1 (#1896): human_actions sev "high" and "critical" show as urgent; OBJECTIVE-WEIGHT-OWNER-ONLY-1 (#1823): a weight revision is ratified only with the owner's emailed-code session (loop token, legacy owner-key cookie and unrecorded ratifications are held as proposed: 403 plus a 'ratify-held' row from the route, back to 'proposed' from the sweep); constraint revisions stay delegable; OBJECTIVE-CARD-PLAIN-1 (#1944): each objective proposal opens with a plain sentence of what it changes and what Ratify, Reject and no decision do; the Open data links name docs/keys/owner-api-keys.md (#1933); 1.21.5 SOURCE-RESTORE-1 (GitHub #608): main's worker.js is hand-written source again; commit 1e97baba (LAND-CODE-FIX-1) had landed the wrangler bundle of 1.21.4 over it (comments and block markers gone, __name2 helpers), which broke deploy-gate and seven offline suites; this file is the 1.21.3 source plus the 1.21.4 change; 1.21.4 OWNER-KEYS-VIEW-1: GET /api/owner/keys lists the active owner_client_keys rows from the private qnfo-identity D1 (owner session or loop token only, like every /api/owner/* read; OWNER-CLIENT-KEY-DRIFT-1, #1886); 1.21.3 FLEET-CMD-Q08-1: command-line ops "style" (standing editorial direction, qnfo-audit q08_editor_notes, read by q08-signal-engine 0.8.4 as authenticated text) and "verdict" (good|flat|no on the q08 article the page link was opened from, qnfo-audit q08_owner_verdicts, weighted 3 in q08 feedbackScan); both owner-session only (emailed code), so a stranger can never steer the publication; 1.21.2 LOOP-SYNC-DEADLINE-1: loopSync stops starting issues after 4 minutes and loopMaybeSync abandons it after 6, so the cron no longer runs into the 15-minute wall limit (#1938, #1942); 1.21.1 BRANCH-HYGIENE-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS measures qnfo-fleet-control 0.4.110's hourly branch sweeper (heartbeat branch-hygiene-tick; counted when it is stalled or its last tick errored); 1.20.3 render-health-watch; 1.20.1 CTL-FROM-NO-QUERY-1: /ctl.js scopes its link with origin + path, never the query string (q08 and companion confirm/unsubscribe pages carry ?t=<token>; a click sent it to /cmd); 1.18.10 WATCHMAKER_OPS measures ERROR-DETAIL-CAPTURE-1 (qnfo-ops 2.38.40: every 30-minute tick copies Workers Logs error events into worker_logs, heartbeat error-capture-tick; an error tick, e.g. a token without the observability read scope, does not prove the run); 1.18.9 GUARD-RCS-CANCELLED-1: guard_rcs reads each guard's latest run that concluded (not cancelled or skipped), so a concurrency-cancelled run no longer raises the security-gap trigger (agent_issues 1797); WATCHMAKER_OPS measures SECRET-CHANGE-WATCH-1 (qnfo-ops 2.38.39: every worker secret change recorded in cloud_ops_events and filed for a consumer check, heartbeat secret-watch-tick); 1.18.8 PAGEVIEWS-HUMAN-1: pageviews_30d and the prior window behind impressions_growth_30d read RUM with the Exclude-bots filter (bot: 0), the filter engaged_human_sessions_28d already uses, so crawler page loads no longer count as readers; the registry formula says so; 1.18.7 GRANT-FOLLOWUP-HONEST-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS grant-followup dates the runner's last run of any status and counts the runs since the last full read with their reason (gmail_pass_unset, errors), instead of reading "never ran" for a runner that runs without GMAIL_PASS; 1.18.6 IPATENT-USAGE-1: the ipatent command and patent questions read iPatent views, searches and drafts by topic (counts only) over SVC_QNFO_IPATENT; 1.18.5 ASK-TRUNCATED-JSON-1: a reply cut at max_tokens is salvaged (answer + complete actions), never shown as raw JSON; max_tokens stays 700 (the open-access cost bound); 1.18.4 ASK-RETRY-1: abandoned asks are answered again by the cron (max 2 tries, 3 per run); fastest model first (glm-5.2, 2s) instead of a 13s timeout on glm-5.3-flash; owner command-line asks in the request history; 1.18.3 REACH-IDEATION-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS measures qnfo-fleet-control's daily reach ideation; 1.18.2 ASCII-SOURCE-1: every non-ASCII character in the source is written as an escape, because the canonical deploy uploads the file as Latin-1 and the live page showed the KPI arrow as mojibake; 1.18.1 OWNER-SURFACE-HONESTY-1 + OBJECTIVE-AUTHORITY-TRUTH-1: an inbox card only for mail that owes the owner a reply, classified by INBOUND-SLA-1's category or the same header/subject rules (a funder's automated receipt such as emails 903 gets no card and is listed as handled), with sender domain, subject and authentication on the public card and the sender's name and address only for the signed-in owner; "fix all the issues" and search-query questions are answered from fleet data with no model and change nothing; a model failure answers from the data instead of failing bare, and the legacy Ask works inside waitUntil so it can no longer be left 'running'; Ask context carries the stored facts (domain, subject, received, auth verdict, classification; name, address and 300 characters for the owner) of the messages a question names; the ratify route records the credential that acted (human_responses.credential), and the apply step stamps ratified_by and objective_revision_applies.via from it ('owner (fleet.qnfo.org, emailed code)', 'delegated (loop token, OWNER-QUEUE-DELEGATION-1)', 'unknown credential'), never assuming the owner; the objective card shows the full statement and rationale; the open-issue digest never echoes a D1 error (public answer; CodeQL js/stack-trace-exposure); 1.18.0 FLEET-UI-2: the dashboard, /cmd and the queue fragment rebuilt in the QNFO design system shared with ask.qwav.tech and papers.qnfo.org (hero verdict with KPI meters, queue cards, investment and system rail, business grid with trend bars, dark mode); same view model, routes, controls and refresh contract; 1.17.8 FLEET-CTL-PRINT-SAFE-1: the /ctl.js link hides in print/PDF and in automated browsers; 1.17.7 PERFORMANCE-LOOP-1: engaged_human_sessions_28d from bot-filtered RUM (STRATEGY s6.3, s9) in the registry refresh; WATCHMAKER_OPS measures qnfo-fleet-control's lever experiments and its five hourly KPIs; 1.17.6 OUTREACH-LEARNER-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS measures qnfo-cloud-ops 1.18.0's outreach learner (daily ol-tick heartbeat; an outreach run with no logged allocation counts as stuck); 1.17.5 SOCIAL-DISTRIBUTION-LEARNER-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS social-learner reads the weekly update ledger of the qnfo-social 0.7.28 distribution learner (social-learner-update-<day>, meta.last_ok; cadence 168h, first due 2026-10-13); 1.17.4 INBOUND-SLA-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS measures qnfo-email-orchestrator's inbound SLA step (counted when its 15-minute runner is stalled or disabled, or a human inbound message is older than 72h with no fleet action); the weekly identity review lists the inbound messages it held without a substantive answer (category and sender domain only); 1.17.3 WATCHMAKER_OPS measures ASK-LOOP-1 (qnfo-ai-search 2.0.1: ask.qwav.tech measured hourly, evaluated, tuned and repaired daily); 1.17.2 WORK-WITH-ME-METRIC-1: the daily reach ingest counts inbound mail tagged [work-with-me:<offer>] (qnfo-gateway WORK-WITH-ME-1) and RUM page views of qnfo.org/work-with-me over 30 days into reach_signals and metric_registry (inbound_contacts_30d, work_with_me_pageviews_30d), shown in GET /api/reach and the portfolio KPIs, watched by WATCHMAKER_OPS; 1.17.1 IMPROVEMENT-LOOP-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS measures qnfo-fleet-control's hourly improvement loop (metric_history, regressions, fix durability); 1.17.0 FLEET-CMD-1: "Ask the fleet" becomes a natural-language command line (pinned on /, full page /cmd, /ctl.js link for any fleet page): instant read commands, plain-English answers with one-tap proposed actions, run in the background and polled (the old panel was wiped by the 10s refresh and its asks hung in status running); actions need an emailed 6-digit code (OWNER_CODE_TO, 12h session, destructive ones need a code from the last 15 min); abandoned running rows swept; no refresh on the ask path; 1.16.5 CODE-TASK-MERGE-RUNNER-1: code-task-merge is run by qnfo-fleet-control 0.4.86 (opens and merges code-loop PRs, hourly heartbeat) and counted only when the runner is stalled or disabled, a PR is stuck, a task needs a person, a pushed branch waits 6h for its PR, a revert failed, or a person merged or closed a code-loop PR after the runner's first ok tick; EVOLVE-HEARTBEAT-1: fleet-defects reads evolveTick's daily heartbeat; 1.16.4 REACH-LOOPS-WATCH-1: WATCHMAKER_OPS measures the delegated identity and reach loops (qnfo-social profile sync, posting and Buffer cross-post, scan, channel audit, engagement; qnfo-cloud-ops engagement, email triage, radar; radar-hub mention radar, job-market watch, events radar); 1.16.3 WATCHMAKER_OPS measures GRANT-FOLLOWUP-1 (qnfo-cloud-ops); 1.16.2 WATCHMAKER_OPS lists OBJECTIVE-CONSTRAINTS-1 (qnfo-fleet-control hourly, owner-ratified goals 41, 43, 57); 1.16.1 WATCHMAKER_OPS measures errata-hub's hourly members (#1747); 1.16.0 Q08-REVIEW-2026-10-31 (#1716): one-shot q08 decision on bot-filtered RUM page views; cadence cut via ops_config q08_max_per_day; 1.15.2 /health capabilities and limitations (#1735); 1.15.1 IDENTITY-WEEKLY-DELEGATED-1: no re-ask cards under the owner's queue delegation; decided leads skipped; 1.15.0 OPEN-ACCESS-1: no token or login to read or Ask; fleet-changing controls off the public page; 1.14.1 TASK-INTENT-INTAKE-1 (1733); 1.14.0 WATCHMAKER-INDEX-1; 1.13.1 OWNER-NOTES-ROUTE-1 files owner tasks and notes as agent_issues; 1.13.0 OBJECTIVE-REVISION-APPLY-1 + OWNER-NOTES-ROUTE-1 + STRATEGY KPI by tag; 1.12.1 IDENTITY-STORE-1 hardening + copy-only sync; owner links refuse claude.ai; 1.12.0 IDENTITY-STORE-1 + IDENTITY-WEEKLY-1; 1.11.1 OWNER-EDIT-1 */ /* 1.50.0 ARCH-OVERDUE-LOOPS-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy, agent_issues 100124): the architecture model now says whether a loop is late against its own schedule. archCadenceMin reads a worker's crons (Cloudflare day-of-week, 1 = Sunday) as the largest gap in minutes over one week; a worker or fleet-exec task is overdue when its last tick is older than that gap plus 120 minutes (the SLA-120M-1 allowance). workers[].cadence_min, beat.overdue, loops[].age_min / cadence_min / overdue, summary.overdue_loops and a top-level overdue list; the page marks the late ones and counts them. A worker with no cron, or a cron that pins a day of the month, is not judged. On 2026-10-19 the model listed qnfo-subscribers (1085 minutes), personal-api (300) and qnfo-gateway (242) beside loops that tick every 5 minutes; all three run daily or weekly crons and none was late. */ /* 1.49.1 ARCH-SELF-MODEL-1 follow-up (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy): the live model listed three workers no layer named (ai-health-prober, fleet-exec, fleet-graph) as unclassified; the first two join the control plane and fleet-graph joins observe. */ /* 1.49.0 DASHBOARD-COST-FEED-1 (2026-10-19, agent_issues 100071, owner directive FULL-PLANE-ACCESS-1, pillar cost): GET /api/cost, the cost area's own read: 30-day run-rate by source (v_cost_run_rate), 14 days of daily spend (cost_daily), Workers AI calls, errors, tokens, neurons and estimated USD by model and by worker over 7 days (ai_call_counters), and every spend budget with headroom (fleet_budget); registered in API_INDEX under area cost, so /api/area/cost lists two feeds. cost-feed.test.mjs. */ /* 1.48.0 ARCH-SELF-MODEL-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; owner directive 2026-10-19: "the system needs to know more about its own architecture (self reference)"): the fleet can now be asked what it is. GET /api/architecture (and /api/architecture/worker/<name>, /table/<name>, /impact/<worker>, ?q=, ?full=1) and the page /architecture answer from the fleet code and live topology graph (FLEET-GRAPH-1, qnfo-graph), the service registry, the heartbeats and the cron table: every worker with its layer, purpose, version, last tick, schedules, routes, databases, service dependencies in both directions and the tables its code reads and writes; the databases, the fleet-exec loops, how a change travels from a work claim to a canaried deploy, the lanes of authority, the registries that hold the fleet's memory, the graph's own findings, and self: the sources it read, the ones it could not, and what it cannot see. Platform area page "Architecture". Open and read-only; names and counts only. */ /* 1.47.0 CREDIT-HALT-ONLY-1 (2026-10-19, owner directive): "AI credit runs out in about N days" is never a queue card and never urgent. The prepaid balance is topped up automatically, so a low balance halts nothing. The only credit card is a balance at or below zero AND gateway calls actually failing on credit in the last 6 hours (llm_gateway_log 402/403 or insufficient/quota/payment/billing/credit errors). A runway is display context only. */ /* 1.46.1 GA4-PROPERTY-MISMATCH-1 (2026-10-19, pillar reach, agent_issues 2935, code task ct_im2935): ga4Ingest read the default property 486253526, which holds 1-2 page views a day for qnfo-publications.pages.dev, while the Zaraz tag G-LV7RHRVW6R on qnfo.org, q08.org, qwav.tech, ipatent.me and empoweringchange.today sits in another property. With no pinned ops_config ga4_property_id it now lists the properties the service account can read (Analytics Admin accountSummaries), reads the web data streams of each, takes the property whose stream carries the measurement id, and saves it as ga4_property_id; when none does, the ledger row ga4-ingest-<day> says so (property_warning) and the default is read. The codeagent draft looked for measurementId on the property summary, which has none, and sat behind a numeric check that was always true. */ /* 1.46.0 KPI-THRESHOLDS-1 + CODE-FLOW-PR-SPLIT-1 + OWNER-REQUEST-RECEIPT-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; agent_issues 2727 and 2941, and the owner-queue metrics owner_queue_fleet_cards_open and owner_dispatched_items_7d): (1) the Metrics tab opens with Service levels: every registered metric against its threshold with a state (BREACH, WARN, NO_READING, OK, NO_THRESHOLD), the time in breach and its area, BREACH first and longest first, and the items past their SLA by class with the age of the oldest (v_metric_trigger_state, metric_registry, metric_history, v_sla_over_120m); GET /api/kpi gains service_levels and sla and loses every last_7d and all_time key (code tasks are one 30-day split, posts per channel 30 days). (2) The Code loop flow splits "closed, unmerged" by what happened to the pull request (code_task_pr_links): closed never opened a PR, PR closed unmerged, and superseded, which stays out of the failure share; one SQL (CODE_OUTCOME_SQL) feeds the flow and the Metrics tab. (3) The card the owner_request_requires_card trigger writes when an owner-request issue closes (source owner-request-issue) is a receipt: listed apart as Closed for you with its issue number, never in the count, the banner or an area queue; the gate already grades such a card "notice" (OWNER-NOTICE-GATE-1, migrations/2026-10-19-owner-notice-gate.sql), so the two metrics do not count it; this release changes only the page. */ /* 1.45.0 AREA-PAGES-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; owner directive 2026-10-18 "refactor the fleet dashboard and API feeds by functional area", repeated 2026-10-19 as "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD BY FUNCTIONAL AREA"): the page half of AREA-FEEDS-1. (1) AREA_REGISTRY is the one table for the six areas; FEED_AREAS, AREA_DEFS, AREA_STARS, AREA_ROOT and AREA_FLOW are derived from it, and it holds what each area's page, tabs, cards, switches and owner-queue cards are. (2) The navigation is the areas (Overview, Cost, Research, Reach, Autonomy, Reliability, Platform, Command line); Metrics, Content and Changelog sit under their area behind a sub-bar. (3) /area/<id> answers for all six areas (/area/platform was a 404): the area's queue cards, constraint and breaches, pipelines, the cards that sat on the overview (Keep investing? under Cost, Progress and the social switch under Reach, System health under Reliability, the owner-queue switch under Autonomy), its reads with their lag, and a live refresh (?frag=1&rev=, 204 while nothing moved). The overview keeps the queue, one row per area (a named bottleneck or a stopped switch shows on the row) and the two headline cards. (4) GET /api by_area and /api/area/<id> name each area's page. (5) AREAS2_CSS was defined and never sent, so the per-area rows, the switch list and the summary link shipped unstyled on 1.44.5; it is in the page head now. (6) /kpi and /api/kpi answered 500 on 1.44.5 (a half-built KPI-THRESHOLDS-1 rebuilt the KPI document as if it were a map of metrics); the route serves the document as computed, and agent_issues 2727 stays open. (7) "AREA-MAP <id>:" banners mark 40 regions of this file by area (README, Code map). Additive except the overview's Pipelines, Progress and Pipeline controls cards, which moved to their areas. */ /* 1.44.7 FALLBACK-HEARTBEAT-PROOF-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy): each scheduled run writes fleet_heartbeat (ok = the guarded fleet_tick fallback insert ran without error), so fm-single-heartbeat proves the second heartbeat producer is alive on a day the primary never misses instead of failing 24 hours after the last fallback tick. */ /* 1.44.4 AREA-FEEDS-5 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; deployment test of 1.44.3): GET /api was the one read that carried an ETag but no body `revision` and no ?since=, so a client that cannot send a header - the reason ?since= exists - could not dedupe the 13 KB index, and the index's own note ("a read with ?since=<revision> answers {not_modified:true}") was false about itself. It now goes through rtRead like every other read: body revision, ?since=, and an ETag over the content (endpoints plus the area grouping) instead of a path-and-schema string. The catalogue row now says "public 15s, ETag". Additive: no field removed, the ETag is strictly stronger. */ /* 1.44.3 AREA-FEEDS-4 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; closeout review of 1.44.2): the area feed omitted `blind`, the list of sources areasModel could not read, while /api/areas carries it. With a table unreadable and no breach rows the feed would have answered breaches:0 and status ok - a false green in the one read whose job is truthful state, which is exactly what AREAS-1 forbids ("an unreadable source is named in blind, never shown as zero"). The feed now carries blind (the same list /api/areas sends) and degraded:true when it is non-empty. Additive only: no field removed, status still agrees with /api/areas so the two cannot disagree. */ /* 1.44.2 AREA-FEEDS-3 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; red-team of the live 1.44.1): GET /api/area/<id> answered 500 {"error":"URI malformed"} for a malformed percent sequence (/api/area/%E0%A4%A, /api/area/%C0%80) because decodeURIComponent throws and the id was decoded unguarded, while every pre-existing read compares the raw path and answers 404. An anonymous poller can send one by accident, so a 500 here is noise in the fleet's own error accounting. The decode is now guarded and an unreadable escape falls back to the raw segment, which takes the 404 path. Everything else red-teamed clean: trailing slash, case, empty and nested ids, ?since= with and without the rev: prefix, ?full=1 size, the revision holding steady while data.age_min moved 22 -> 23 over 65 s (the nested clock field really is excluded), and no owner field in the body. No route or field removed. */ /* 1.44.1 AREA-FEEDS-2 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; two defects found by verifying 1.44.0 in production, both mine): (a) the page claim was wrong - humanHtml renders areas through focusRowsHtml, not areaSectionsHtml, which is dead code with no call site, so editing it changed nothing anyone sees; that edit is reverted and the link that was actually missing (an area row to its machine feed) is added to focusRowsHtml in both branches, clean areas included. (b) by_area[].live meant "every member read is live" while the feed's own live:true means "this feed is computed at request time"; two meanings under one name in one response, so the grouping field is renamed all_members_live and the feed keeps live. No route, field or behaviour removed. */ /* 1.44.0 AREA-FEEDS-1 (2026-10-18, owner directive "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD AND API FEEDS BY FUNCTIONAL AREA. FEEDS SHALL ALWAYS BE UPDATED AUTOMATICALLY FROM LIVE DATA AND REAL-TIME"): the read surface is now organised by functional area, as data, not as a convention. (a) Every row of API_INDEX names the one functional area it serves (cost, research, reach, autonomy, reliability, platform), whether it is computed live at request time or from the rebuilt fleet state, and the worst-case lag a client must assume (source, lag_s). GET /api returns that grouping as areas + by_area, so a client can enumerate an area's feeds instead of guessing. (b) GET /api/area/<id> is one functional area as its own feed: its star metric and trend, its breaches ranked, the constraint it is believed to be stuck behind, its pipelines, and the reads that belong to it with their declared lag. It is computed from D1 at request time and revalidates like every other read. (c) Fixed a shadowed route: GET /api/ga4 answered 500 "GA4 property ID not set" live because an older handler for the same path stood in front of the GA4-DATA-API-1 ledger read and called the retired stub helpers; the stale handler and its two dead helpers are removed, so the ledger read answers and revalidates. (d) A snapshot-backed read now refreshes itself: /api/actions and /api/integration read fleet_dashboard_state without ever scheduling a rebuild (only /api/state did), so a poller could keep receiving a 15-minute-old answer; both go through freshState, and /health states which reads are snapshot-backed and which are live. (e) the area feed states the age of the oldest metric it reports (data.age_min) because those metrics are refreshed by other workers and on 2026-10-18 ranged from minutes to 18 hours; RT_CLOCK strips age_min at any depth, so the age never moves the revision while the values and the count do. No route removed, no field removed. */ /* 1.43.2 ALL-READS-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "FIX ALL AUTOMATICALLY"): the last two open reads join the same contract, so every open read now revalidates. GET /api/loop and GET /api/changes kept returning no-store with no validator; both now go through rtRead and carry a revision, an ETag and ?since= (on /api/changes ?since= still means the timestamp it always meant: a revision is eight hex characters and can never equal an ISO date, so the two meanings cannot collide). With this the whole open read surface - 18 endpoints plus /health - answers the same way. */ /* 1.43.1 KPI-CLOCK-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; COMPACT-READS-1 follow-up, found by verifying the deploy): /api/kpi answered 200 instead of 304 because its body carries as_of and activity_as_of, built from Date.now() on every build, so two isolates (or one cache expiry) could never agree on a revision. The route suite missed it because kpiData hands back an isolate-cached object, so both calls in one process saw the same as_of; rt-api.test.mjs now slices the digest block and asserts a clock field never moves the revision and a content field always does (J2-J6), which fails without this fix. Both fields join RT_CLOCK. No behaviour change beyond the 304. */ /* 1.43.0 COMPACT-READS-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "FIX ALL AUTOMATICALLY" after the RT-API-1 report): the three items that report listed as deliberately not done or as a sharp edge. (a) GET /api/areas was 101 KB and 55 KB of it was fullLever, the same text the clipped lever already carries at ten times the length; the API now sends the compact row and keeps the whole source text behind ?full=1, schema_version fleet-areas/v3 (the page is untouched: it renders from areasModel, not from the API). (b) GET /api/changelog sent all 267 releases (164 KB) to every poller that wanted the newest ten; it is now one page of 20 with ?limit=1..200, ?offset= and ?limit=all, schema_version changelog/v2, and it revalidates like the other reads instead of no-store. (c) the digest no longer strips fields by a blanket name rule: RT_CLOCK is the explicit list of clock fields and everything else is part of the identity, and rt-api.test.mjs now mutates a NON-clock field of every revalidating read and requires the ETag to move, so over-stripping fails the suite instead of silently serving a stale 304. (d) ?since=<revision> is now on /api/areas, /api/human and /api/changelog as well as /api/live, every revalidating read returns X-Revision, and a body field `revision`, so a client that cannot send a header can still dedupe. No route removed; no field removed without a parameter that restores it. */ /* 1.42.1 RT-API-FRAGMENT-REV-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; RT-API-1 follow-up, found by verifying the deploy): the first deploy revalidated the JSON reads exactly as designed (/api/live, /api/areas, /api/human answered 304 with no body) but the page fragment did not: the same code path sets an ETag and the edge serves X-Generated-At while the ETag never arrives on text/html, so the poll's If-None-Match could not match and it kept re-downloading 12 KB. The fragment now carries X-Content-Revision and accepts it back as ?rev=, answering 204 with no body while nothing has changed; If-None-Match and 304 remain honoured. No other route is touched. */ /* 1.42.0 RT-API-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD AND REAL-TIME APIS"): measured on the live worker before the change, no read answered with an ETag, every read answered Cache-Control: no-store and GET /api was a 404, so a poller could neither ask "did anything change?" nor enumerate what it may call; /api/areas alone was 122 KB and the page re-downloaded and re-rendered its fragment every 60 s whether or not anything had moved. Now (a) GET /api is the read API's own index: every public read with its auth, schema_version, cache policy and what makes it change (API_INDEX, one table instead of scattered knowledge); (b) GET /api/live is one consolidated snapshot for pollers (state verdict and probe counts, queue open/urgent/oldest, the five areas with their constraint and lever, the invest verdict, a content revision) so a poll is one call instead of four; (c) a read carries a weak ETag computed over its CONTENT digest (time-derived fields excluded, or nothing would ever revalidate), honours If-None-Match with 304 and an empty body, and states a cache policy: public reads may be reused 15 s, a response carrying owner fields never may (private, no-store, Vary on the credential); (d) /api/areas, /api/human and /api/live answer 304, and /api/live also accepts ?since=<revision> for clients that cannot send a header; (e) the page's 60 s poll sends the fragment's ETag and on 304 leaves the DOM untouched (no re-render, no lost focus or scroll); (f) /health advertises api-index, live-snapshot and conditional-reads. No route was removed and no payload field was dropped. */ /* 1.41.0 GA4-DATA-API-1 (2026-10-18, pillar reach): the GA4 stubs are replaced by a real Data API read (service account GA4_SA_KEY, RS256 JWT in WebCrypto) into reach_signals source ga4 once a day, ledger cloud_ops_events ga4-ingest-<day>, GET/POST /api/ga4, watchmaker op ga4-ingest; a refused grant writes its reason, never a zero. */ /* 1.40.1 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-2 (2026-10-17, pillar security/observability): the request and cron error catches now log at error severity with structured JSON (they previously returned the error without logging, so unhandled request/cron failures were invisible in the logs); the remaining string-concatenated console.log/console.error calls are converted to structured JSON.stringify so logs are searchable. No control-flow changes. */ /* 1.40.0 REACH-DIRECT-TRAFFIC-1 (2026-10-17, pillar reach; owner directive: see which papers and links are accessed directly from Cloudflare analytics/logs, not just social-media clicks): the /kpi Reach section gains "Top papers, opened directly" and "Top links, opened directly" (7 days), read from reach_signals source cf-rum-human (Cloudflare Web Analytics RUM page loads, bots excluded) grouped per paper slug and per page, excluding the fleet's own surfaces so the owner's own browsing is not reader traffic. This is the direct-traffic complement to the social-post click counts under "Most popular posts". */ /* 1.39.2 OWNER-VOICE-CAP-PARITY-1 (2026-10-17, pillar reach, agent_issues 2729): portfolioWeeklyCap falls back to PORTFOLIO_SOCIAL_WEEKLY_CAP_DEFAULT = 21, the cap qnfo-social weeklyCap runs (PAPER-ROLLUP-1), instead of the literal 2; the 2 failed the cadence check on any normal week, so the 05:00Z guard wrote social_paused=1 on 10-15, 10-16 and 10-17. wrangler.toml sets SOCIAL_ALLOW_Q08=1 so the 1.38.3 q08-link rule does not flag links Q08-OPEN-1 made allowed. // 1.39.1 (2026-10-17): a lever is clipped on a word boundary, so it can no longer end mid-word ("about 4k neur..."), and the root-cause sentence names the titles it is expected to clear in quotes. */ /* 1.39.0 AREAS-2 (2026-10-17, pillar autonomy; owner directive EXECUTE REFACTOR: organise the dashboard by functional area and action, not by data source, without overload, extraneous information or redundancy): the 15-row breach wall and the five tiles are replaced by five constraint rows, each carrying the cost of its constraint, its owner, its lever, the lever type (code loop / your decision / fleet loops) and its remedy history; every other breach moves to a deterministic /area/<id> page, which replaces 15 per-row Diagnose model calls; AREA_ROOT promotes the known root cause (verification monoculture) over the tautological probe count and names the breaches it explains; a Pipeline controls card shows kill-switch state and when it was set; the duplicate Subscribers progress bar is removed; GET /api/areas carries schema_version fleet-areas/v2. */ /* 1.38.0 AREAS-1 (2026-10-17, pillar autonomy; owner request: refactor the dashboard around functional areas and action items, with bottlenecks and progress, without overload or redundancy): five area tiles (cost, research and content, reach and growth, autonomy loop, reliability and security) each with one star metric and its 14-day trend replace the four-number strip; breaching metrics are listed per area, worst three first (priority, then gap), each with owner, sparkline, direction and a Diagnose control that fills the command line; a Pipelines panel shows issue age, code loop, research queue and outreach with the bottleneck stage named; the Open data card and the business-case section are removed (every figure is a tile, a verdict reason or a sparkline); GET /api/areas. / 1.37.6 SCHEMA-ALTER-PROBE (2026-10-14, pillar core; the qnfo-ops 2.48.2 pattern, PR 1258): liveScheduled reads pragma_table_info(worker_schedules) and runs ALTER ... ADD COLUMN created_on only when the column is missing, so no failed D1 statement reaches Workers telemetry on each call; the schema block's outer catch logs. */ /* 1.37.5 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-qnfo-fleet-dashboard-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2532, pillar security): the 14 swallowed catches log; the nine server-side ones write console.error JSON with worker and where (the ALTER TABLE on worker_schedules skips the expected duplicate-column error), and the five in the browser scripts write console.debug. Control flow unchanged. */ /* 1.37.4 FLEET-JOURNEYS-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2554, pillar core): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists fleet-journeys, the qnfo-fleet-control journey tick (journey_runs runner cf, every 20 minutes). */ /* 1.37.3 SLA-120M (2026-10-14, owner directive: no SLA exceeds 120 minutes, 10-minute resolution): the loop-signal warn SLA is 120 min (was 720) and its breach comment speaks in minutes; the watchmaker stuck limits are in minutes (stuck_min) and every SLA-type limit is 120 min (inbound mail 72 h, code-loop PR green 6 h and waiting 48 h, attention decisions 240 h, transformation levers 24 h, q08 review and intents 48 h); lookback windows keep their length (Gmail runs and person merges 30 days, outreach-learner allocation 48 h). */ /* 1.37.2: the outreach-learner stuck count excludes jr-outreach-lookup-* runs, as it excludes jr-outreach-learner-*. */ /* 1.37.1 OUTREACH-LOOKUP-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-14, pillar reach): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists outreach-lookup, the qnfo-cloud-ops email lookup that runs every 120 minutes (SLA-120M-3). */ /* 1.37.0 CONTENT-PIPELINE-1 (2026-10-14, pillars research and reach; owner directive "visibility onto the content creation process end-to-end on fleet dashboard"): a Content tab (/content, JSON /api/content, open) shows every idea, paper, revision, erratum and post on one production line from qnfo-audit v_content_pipeline (stage, state, time in stage, SLA verdict), with papers.qnfo.org release-gate verdicts (living-paper) and q08.org signal-to-piece gates (q08-signal); the cron writes metric papers_live_failing_release_gate hourly. */ /* 1.36.1 COMPAT-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-13, agent_issues 2412, pillar autonomy): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists compat-exposure, the qnfo-ops worker_compat_state snapshot (cadence 12h). ATTENTION-STOP-GRADE-1 (agent_issues 2467): a STOP decision is graded on its prediction, the other social channels' pooled 7-day attention per item, not on its own channel, whose share it set to 0 (that read 0 -> 0, flat, for every STOP). */ /* 1.36.0 MAIL-REPLY-INSTRUCTION-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner screenshot "Fail."): "reply 984 always show me draft first before sending" was offered for sending as the email text. reply <id> <text> now sends the text as written only when it reads as an email (greeting, several lines or a sign-off); otherwise the text is the owner's instruction and the fleet writes a complete draft of that reply, shown first with Send reply. The Reply button on inbox cards and in mail <id> runs that draft directly. */ /* 1.35.0 MAIL-DRAFT-COMPLETE-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner: "when prompted the system must generate full, complete, ready-to-send drafts. do not burden the user with manual effort or input"): drafts no longer carry [[FILL]] gaps. The fleet reads the facts itself (the qnfo.org public profile, the owner identity document in the private store, and the GitHub record of any file or commit the thread names) and the model writes a complete reply, including any requested statement, from those records only; a sentence with a title, unit or placeholder no record supports is dropped in code; every draft carries Send reply. */ /* 1.34.0 MAIL-DRAFT-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner: "NEVER INVENT TITLES OR BUSINESS UNITS OR FABRICATE ANY OTHER INFORMATION ... REQUIRING MULTIPLE BACK-AND-FORTH PROMPTS"): a request to draft a reply no longer goes to the general question prompt. It finds the message by address, id or inbox card, gives the model the stored thread and only the owner's name, turns every fact on record nowhere into a [[FILL: ...]] gap (a title or unit the thread lacks is replaced in code), writes the sign-off in code (name and sending address), shows real line breaks, and offers Edit, then send (the full reply in the command line, up to 8000 characters) and, with no gap left, Send reply; "send it" sends the last draft; a reply holding a gap is refused. */ /* 1.33.0 OWNER-DOC-API-1 (2026-10-12, agent_issues 2373 EMAIL-VOICE-DRAFTER-1, pillar personal): GET and POST /api/owner/doc/<key> let a fleet worker read an owner document and propose a new version (or create a seed the owner then edits) through this worker, the only one bound to the private store; the caller is a service binding that names itself in props (qnfo-email), the loop token or the owner session. 1.31.3: main (1.30.1-kpi) merged into this branch; the version moves so the push is ahead. 1.31.2: the inbox reply and spam paths log their caught errors to Workers Logs (WORKERS-STANDARDS-GUARD-1). 1.31.1: INBOX-REPLY-1 (below) and OUTREACH-SLA-CYCLE-1 ship in the same PR as 1.31.0; the version moves so each push is ahead. */ /* 1.31.0 DASHBOARD-FULL-ACCESS-1 (2026-10-12, pillar autonomy; owner: "the fleet dashboard is my one-stop assistant to the Quniverse and shall have full access to all resources"): every ask since 09:33Z answered "the model service answered HTTP 401": a secret added to this worker in the Cloudflare dashboard (GA4_SA_KEY) made a version that copied the bindings without props, so SVC_QNFO_AI lost caller = qnfo-fleet-dashboard and qnfo-ai refused it. A model leg that qnfo-ai refuses (401/403/5xx or a network error) now runs the same Workers AI model through the new direct AI binding, so a lost prop or a router outage no longer silences the command line. The console gains mail (search the inbox, read a message in full; owner session), service bindings to qnfo-email, qnfo-cloud-ops and qnfo-fleet-control for call, and sql reads of q08 and the private identity D1 (owner session; identity stays read-only). info shows the signed-in owner up to 1500 characters of each message and tells a visitor to sign in for subject and text. MAIL-ASK-1 (same day, owner: "what do they say? don't waste my time"): "WHAT'S UP WITH RESEARCHLETTERS EMAILS?" and the follow-up "AND WHAT DO THEY SAY?" reached the model with no message at all (MAIL_ASK_RX had no plurals, "researchletters" did not name theresearchletters.org, a follow-up named no sender); plurals, say/said/content, a card domain typed without "the" or the TLD, and a follow-up with mail cards in the queue now read the messages, and the owner's context carries 1500 characters of each (was 300). A journal call for papers ("We invite your research article", "submit your full-length article", "fits our journal scope", an ISSN line) is a solicitation, not a person owed a reply (MAIL_SOLICIT_RX, parity with qnfo-cloud-ops 1.25.2), so it leaves the owner queue and is listed as handled. INBOX-REPLY-1 (same day, owner: "THE FLEET DASHBOARD IS THE QNFO INBOX"): inbox cards carry Reply (fills "reply <id> " in the command line), Read and Spam; "reply <id> <text>" sends through qnfo-email /send as the address the mail was written to (rowan.quni@qnfo.org by default) with "Re: <subject>", marks the message replied and closes its queue rows (fresh code: it speaks in the owner's name); "spam <id> ..." marks messages spam and adds the sender to email_sender_policy junk; the card no longer says "Open the qnfo inbox". */ /* 1.30.1: hosts fold to their property by domain, probe clicks left out, 64-character campaign tags matched, ingest notes carry no upstream error text. 1.30.0 FLEET-KPI-1 (owner request 2026-10-12, pillar reach): a Metrics tab (/kpi, JSON /api/kpi, open to everyone) computed from qnfo-audit at request time: open issues by priority and category, opened and closed per day, median age, oldest, closed with evidence in 24 h and 7 d, code-loop tasks; bot-filtered page views per property and day, outreach actions and events per channel and day, posts per channel, the most popular posts with links, subscribers, mentions and citations; the Buffer channels, posts and per-post statistics; a value nothing measures is null and shown as not measured; the daily reach ingest adds a per-host row with page loads and visits. */ /* 1.29.4 CMD-AI-TIMEOUT-1 (agent_issues 2316): cmdModelCall sends timeout_ms just under its own abort and names a 504 router_timeout; WATCHMAKER-TICK-LEDGER-1 (2363): tick-sequence-ledger entry, duplicate dangling-bindings-census entry removed; REPORT-CARD-CATCHUP-1 (2054): the weekly card is caught up on any tick and deduped only against measured rows. */ /* 1.29.3 WORKERS-BEST-PRACTICES-1: every LOOP_TOKEN check (6 POST routes + the machine flag) uses constEq instead of !==/===. */ /* 1.29.2 MODEL-ID-AUDIT-1 (2026-10-11): qnfo-ai routes only the ids in its MODELS table; llama-3.3-70b-instruct-fp8-fast is not one, so the third ask leg silently became a second deepseek-v4-flash. Order is now glm-5.3-flash, deepseek-v4-flash-wa, gpt-oss-120b: the Workers AI legs probe at 2-3 s through qnfo-ai, the DeepSeek API leg at 13 s. */ /* 1.29.1 CMD-ASK-BUDGET-1 (2026-10-11): 1.29.0 raised CMD_AI_BUDGET_MS to 40 s, but the job runs in ctx.waitUntil, which the runtime cancels 30 s after the response, so the first live ask (cmd-517f7562d37f1041ef0a) never wrote its fallback and stayed running until the page gave up at 45 s; the budget is back under that limit (24 s, hedge 4 s) and a suite asserts it stays below 28 s. 1.29.0 CMD-ASK-FAST-1 (2026-10-11, pillar autonomy; owner: "Fix fleet command line"): every command-line and Ask question since 2026-10-06 ended in the stored-data fallback ("The AI model did not answer just now (it timed out)", 7 of the last 7 rows): ASK_MODELS named glm-5.2, a model qnfo-ai no longer lists, so the first call burned its slot and glm-5.3-flash, asked second with the remaining budget, timed out at 13 s. The models are now raced with a staggered start (ASK_HEDGE_MS, first answer wins; each failure names its model in cmd_log.error), the budget is 40 s, and a question about Q08 reads published_pieces from the q08-signal D1 (new read binding 